  "summary": "Form-fitting dress with high collar and side slits that evolved from Manchu women's clothing",
  "description": "Features a form-fitting silhouette with high collar, side slits, and frog buttons. Developed in 1920s Shanghai as a fusion of traditional Manchu elements with Western tailoring techniques, representing modern Chinese femininity.",
  "culturalContext": "The Shanghai-tailored qipao (1920s-1940s) transformed Manchu robe into modern Chinese glamour: Hong Kong and Shanghai tailors (SAM's, Lin Xiangling) fitted high-collared, princess-seamed silk through \"hot fire\" iron shaping, with Peking-knot frog closures and hand-rolled hems. It dressed the calendar-girl modern woman, survived Maoist ban in Hong Kong tailoring houses, and returned as wedding-and-diplomacy dress; the Shanghai qipao is the Republic-era image of Chinese femininity worldwide.",
